Feature: Plans
  A Financial Plan is the organization's plan for one year. It holds one Operations Plan and a
  Program Plan for any of the organization's program areas, and the P&L they make together. A
  Program Plan covers one program area: what it offers and when, the prices, the schedule, who works
  it, and the budget. Plan Writers plan it. The Operations Plan covers what the program
  areas share, such as the office, the boats, and the year's rates. It also plans the products that
  belong to no program area: merchandise, storage, and charters. It also holds each program area's
  storage rent, such as the dinghy float, which is still charged to the area.

  A Financial Plan is worked on as a whole. It starts as a draft, and planners can change any of
  its plans while it's a draft. When it's ready, a Plan Admin freezes it, and all of its plans are frozen
  together, with their P&L. A frozen Financial Plan never changes, apart from its name, its note, and its date, so
  the organization can always look back at what it agreed to. To change the plan after that, a planner starts a new Financial
  Plan from an earlier one, its parent, and the new one begins as a copy of it. A year can have
  several drafts at once, so planners can weigh options side by side, such as two drafts started
  from the same Financial Plan, one with two coaches and one with three. Each Financial Plan has a
  name, and a note that says what it was for, such as "Reviewed with the board". Planners are the
  people with the Plan Reader, Plan Writer, or Plan Admin policy, which they get from their groups.

  A Plan Writer can start a draft Financial Plan, and change any Program Plan in any draft, for
  every program area. A Plan Admin also sets the rules everyone plans within: they can change the
  Operations Plan in a draft, such as wages, rates, terms, and shared costs. They also freeze a
  Financial Plan, make one active, and archive one. A Plan Writer can see the Operations Plan but
  can't change it. Which people have which policy is up to their groups.

  A Plan Admin makes a Financial Plan active from a date. The plan in force on a day is the
  active one for that year with the latest date on or before that day, and it stays in force until
  the next one's date. So a re-plan for the fall can take over on September 1, and a plan can be
  made active ahead of time. A draft can be active: Plan Writers keep changing it while it's
  in force, and its P&L keeps up. When the board approves it, a Plan Admin freezes it, and it stays
  in force. A Plan Admin can change any plan's date, earlier or later, to fix a mistake. The plan in
  force can't be archived.

  Each offering in a plan is of a catalog offering: a named offering of a program, such as "J-Pod ·
  fall practices", which Clubspot's offerings are linked to as well. So Clubspot's sign-ups find
  their offering in whichever Financial Plan is in force. An offering's
  activities, such as practices and regattas, stay the same ones in a Financial Plan started from
  another.

  The P&L, the profit and loss for the year, is worked out from a Financial Plan's plans together.
  It shows each offering, each program area, and the organization as a whole. A draft's P&L is
  worked out again a moment after every change to it, whether the change was made in the app, in
  the Data Studio, or by an AI. A frozen Financial Plan keeps the P&L it had when it was frozen, and
  that never changes either. Each line of a frozen P&L says in plain words what it is.
  Shared costs are the costs that several offerings share, such as the office. Each program pays
  toward them at its indirect cost rate, a share of its direct costs. A Plan Admin sets each program's rate in the plan, from the year before,
  and it stays fixed for the year, so one program's year never changes what another pays, or its
  director's bonus. What the rates leave unallocated of the shared costs stays with the organization. Some charges are set as
  a share of revenue, such as scholarships or card fees. The Operations Plan lists each one, with
  its rate and the kinds of product it applies to. Revenue from programs and other sales pays for
  the organization's running costs. Fundraising pays for capital, such as a new coach boat, so
  capital is never charged to a program area.

  CSC pays program bonuses per term, so the P&L also shows each program area's P&L for each term
  the Operations Plan lists, such as Summer 2027. Offerings in no term, such as Adult Learn to Sail,
  are shown on their own, as one more group. A director's bonus is paid on each term's net, and on
  the net of the offerings in no term for the year.

  Each Program Plan sets two numbers for each of its programs: the staff level that coaches it, such
  as Instructor, and how many sailors each coach can take. These are the program's
  settings, and two drafts can try different ones. Each boat class says the most sailors one boat
  can safely take. The asset list says which boats the organization has on each day
  (assets.feature), so every plan counts the same boats. The Operations Plan sets each boat class's
  rates for the year: fuel and upkeep for each hour one of the organization's boats is on the water,
  and a charter rate for each day one is chartered.

  Each plan can also hold the planner's notes: a list of short notes on what they want that the
  plan's numbers don't capture, for their AI to follow when it fills in the plan. Each note is one
  line of at most 280 characters. A program has its own notes, such as "if there are more than 12
  Guppies, add a Junior Instructor", or "Guppies can sail the Harpoon, up to 10 kids, instead of
  Sailqubes". A Program Plan's notes cover all of its programs, such as "J-Pod and K-Pod share
  coaches on Wednesdays". The Operations Plan's notes cover the whole organization, such as "keep
  one coach boat ashore for repairs on summer weekdays". The P&L never reads the notes.

  The schedule is planned in blocks. A block is one or more activities on one date that share
  their coaches and boats, such as Guppies and Minnows on the morning of July 13. Each block lists
  its staff (the people who coach it) and its boats of each class, sailboats and powerboats such as
  coach boats: the organization's own, the ones it charters, and the ones sailors bring. A block runs from its first activity's start to its last activity's end.
  Nothing works out how many coaches or boats a program needs. The planner, or their AI, decides that
  from the program settings and the notes.

  Each offering has the most sailors it takes, and the number the planner expects to sign up. The
  P&L costs every coach and boat a block is given, however many sailors sign up: CSC pays a whole
  coach and RIB for every practice.
  Revenue and the costs per sailor follow the sailors expected.
  A planner sees two numbers for each offering: the most sailors its block can take, and the fewest
  sailors at which it pays for itself.

  A planner is warned when the blocks break a limit: more of the organization's boats of a class out
  at once than it has that day, or too few of its boats of a class for every offering to fill. An
  activity with no staff isn't a warning: a program such as open boating runs without one. Each warning says which plan and date it's about. A full program needs
  sailboats enough for all its sailors, each boat taking its class's most sailors, and a coach boat
  for each coach: each coach can safely take the program's number of sailors. Any of the organization's
  powerboats can be a coach boat, and a boat of another class a program can sail can stand in for
  one it's given, if it's spare that day. Too few boats if every offering fills is a warning; too few for
  the sailors expected is an error, since the plan can't run as it is. The boats that
  sailors bring count, and so do the boats a block charters, since the organization can charter
  more of a class it doesn't own. The planner isn't warned about coaches, since CSC can always hire
  more. A program's staff level is what the planner aims for, not a hard rule: any coach in the
  block counts, and so does a salaried coach, such as a director.

  A planner is also warned about each Program Plan line that moves a cost off the area's programs:
  a line left out of the split of shared costs, a line charged to the shared costs or to the
  organization, or a line for capital. A Plan Admin looks over these before they make a
  plan active. Lines in the Operations Plan never get these warnings, because only a Plan Admin
  changes the Operations Plan.

  A planner can ask their AI to fill in the blocks. It works in a new draft Financial Plan, started
  from the one the planner names. It tells the planner what it changed and which warnings are left,
  and gives the draft a short note, such as "Allocated Summer weeks 3 to 5". If the planner likes
  the draft, they freeze it. If not, they delete it.

  The Operations Plan also lists closed days, such as Labor Day, so that no one plans a practice on
  one by mistake.

  Plans include wages and bonus rates. Wages are what each staff level in the catalog is paid per
  hour, $0 for a volunteer, and what each salaried position is paid per year. A salaried coach can also be paid by the hour for
  coaching, on top of their salary, at their own hourly rate. Bonus rates set the bonus a director
  earns on the products they run: a share of those products' net, the money they make after their
  costs. Every bonus is in the Operations Plan, which a Plan Admin sets. Anyone who can
  see plans sees all of these. To change something means to add, edit, or delete it.

  The Operations Plan also lists the payroll taxes the organization pays on its staff, each the way
  it's collected, and each its own line on the P&L. Most are a share of wages, such as Social
  Security, Medicare, and Washington's unemployment tax. L&I, Washington's workers' comp, is an amount
  for each hour worked: each hour a coach is paid for, and each of a salaried person's paid hours.
  Volunteers are paid nothing, so their hours cost no L&I, and a bonus pays for no hours, so it costs
  none either. A tax on wages is charged on every wage, with no yearly cap on each person's wages,
  such as Social Security's.

  Rule: A Financial Plan holds one Operations Plan, and a Program Plan for any of the program areas

    Example: A Financial Plan can start with only an Operations Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      When they start a Financial Plan for next year with only an Operations Plan
      Then the Financial Plan is a draft, with the Operations Plan in it

    Example: A Financial Plan can't be frozen without an Operations Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan with a Program Plan but no Operations Plan
      When they try to freeze the Financial Plan
      Then they're told that the Financial Plan needs an Operations Plan
      And the Financial Plan is still a draft

  Rule: A Financial Plan holds at most one Program Plan for each program area

    Example: A second Program Plan for the same program area
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan with a Program Plan for Youth Racing
      When they try to add another Program Plan for Youth Racing to it
      Then they're told that the Financial Plan already has a Program Plan for Youth Racing

  Rule: Freezing a Financial Plan freezes all of its plans together

    Example: The Operations Plan and both Program Plans are frozen with the Financial Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan with an Operations Plan and two Program Plans
      When they freeze the Financial Plan
      Then the Financial Plan is frozen, and none of its three plans can change

  Rule: A year can have several draft Financial Plans at once

    Example: Two options for the same year
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan for next year named "Two coaches"
      When they start another draft Financial Plan for next year named "Three coaches"
      Then next year has both drafts

  Rule: A new Financial Plan starts as a copy of the one it's started from

    Example: The new Financial Plan has the same offerings and prices
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" with an offering at $1,000 a sailor
      When they start a new Financial Plan from "Budget"
      Then the new Financial Plan has the same offering at $1,000 a sailor
      And its parent is "Budget"

    Example: Changing the new Financial Plan doesn't change its parent
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" with an offering at $1,000 a sailor
      And they started a new Financial Plan from "Budget"
      When they change the offering's price to $1,200 a sailor in the new Financial Plan
      Then "Budget" still has the offering at $1,000 a sailor

  Rule: Next year's Financial Plan can start from this year's, and starts empty

    Next year's dates differ, such as the weeks schools are out, so nothing is copied. The planner's
    AI fills in next year's plan from the one it started from.

    Example: Starting next year's plan from this year's
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" with an offering at $1,000 a sailor
      When they start a Financial Plan for the year after from "Budget"
      Then its parent is "Budget"
      And the new Financial Plan has no plans

    Example: A plan can't start from one two years before
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" with an offering at $1,000 a sailor
      When they try to start a Financial Plan for two years after from "Budget"
      Then they're told a Financial Plan starts from one for its own year or the year before

  Rule: Each Program Plan sets its programs' staff levels and how many sailors each coach can take

    Example: A new draft tries fewer sailors per coach
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" where Guppies have a coach for every 10 sailors
      And they started a new Financial Plan from "Budget"
      When they change Guppies to a coach for every 8 sailors in the new Financial Plan
      Then "Budget" still has Guppies with a coach for every 10 sailors

  Rule: An offering and its activities stay the same ones in a Financial Plan started from it

    Example: A practice is the same practice in the new Financial Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" with a practice on June 6
      When they start a new Financial Plan from "Budget"
      Then its practice on June 6 is the same practice as the one in "Budget"

  Rule: An offering can be in one of the terms the Operations Plan lists, or in none

    Example: An offering in the summer term
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan whose Operations Plan has a term named "Summer 2027", with an offering
      When they put the offering in "Summer 2027"
      Then the offering is in "Summer 2027"

    Example: An offering in no term, such as Adult Learn to Sail
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan whose offering is in a term
      When they take the offering out of its term
      Then the offering is in no term

  Rule: The plan in force on a day is the active one with the latest date on or before it

    Example: A re-plan for the fall takes over on its date
      Given a Financial Plan named "Budget", active from July 1
      And one named "Fall re-plan", active from September 1
      Then "Budget" is in force on August 31
      And "Fall re-plan" is in force on September 1

    Example: A plan made active ahead of time isn't in force yet
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a Financial Plan named "Budget", in force today
      And a draft Financial Plan named "Spring re-plan"
      When they make "Spring re-plan" active from next month
      Then "Budget" is still in force today
      And "Spring re-plan" is in force from next month

  Rule: A draft can be active, and Plan Writers keep changing it while it's in force

    Example: A Plan Writer changes the draft in force
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan in force today, with an offering at $1,000 a sailor
      When they change the offering's price to $1,200 a sailor
      Then the draft is still in force, and its P&L has the offering at $1,200 a sailor

    Example: Freezing the draft in force keeps it in force
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan in force today
      When they freeze it
      Then it's frozen, and still in force

  Rule: A Plan Admin can change any plan's date

    Example: Moving a frozen plan's date earlier
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a frozen Financial Plan active from September 1
      When they change its date to August 1
      Then it's in force from August 1

    Example: Two Financial Plans for a year can't be active from the same date
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a Financial Plan named "Budget", active from July 1
      And a draft Financial Plan named "Fall re-plan"
      When they make "Fall re-plan" active from July 1
      Then they're told that another Financial Plan for the year is active from that date

  Rule: The plan in force can't be archived

    Example: Archiving the plan in force
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a Financial Plan named "Budget", in force today
      When they try to archive "Budget"
      Then they're told that the plan in force can't be archived

    Example: Archiving a plan another has taken over from
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a Financial Plan named "Budget", active from last month
      And one named "Re-plan", in force today
      When they archive "Budget"
      Then "Budget" is archived

  Rule: A Plan Writer can start a draft and change any Program Plan in it, but not the Operations Plan

    Example: A Plan Writer starts a Financial Plan from another
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      Then they can start a Financial Plan from another

    Example: A Plan Writer changes a Program Plan in a draft someone else started
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan someone else started
      Then they can change its Program Plans
      But they can't change its Operations Plan

    Example: A Plan Writer can't freeze a Financial Plan, make one active, or archive one
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan
      Then they can't freeze it, make it active, or archive it

  Rule: A Plan Admin can change the Operations Plan in a draft, and freeze, make active, and archive a Financial Plan

    Example: A Plan Admin changes the Operations Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan someone else started
      Then they can change its Program Plans
      And they can change its Operations Plan

    Example: A Plan Admin makes a draft active, freezes it, and archives another
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan
      Then they can make it active from a date, and freeze it
      And they can archive a Financial Plan that isn't active

  Rule: Bonuses are set only in the Operations Plan

    Example: A bonus can't go in a Program Plan, even for an admin
      Given a user with the Admin role
      And a draft Financial Plan
      When they add a bonus to one of its Program Plans
      Then they're told that bonuses go in the Operations Plan

  Rule: A planner is warned about a Program Plan line that moves a cost off the area's programs

    Scenario Outline: A Program Plan line that moves a cost
      Given a draft Financial Plan whose Program Plan has a line <that>
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that the line moves a cost off the area's programs

      Examples:
        | that                                    |
        | left out of the split of shared costs   |
        | charged to the shared costs             |
        | charged to the organization             |
        | for capital                             |

    Example: The same line in the Operations Plan
      Given a draft Financial Plan whose Operations Plan has a line left out of the split of shared costs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about the line

  Rule: A frozen Financial Plan never changes, even for an admin

    Example: A Plan Writer can't change a frozen Financial Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan
      Then they can't change the Financial Plan or any of its plans
      And they can't turn the Financial Plan back into a draft
      And they can't delete the Financial Plan

    Example: An admin can't change a frozen Financial Plan either
      Given a user with the Admin role
      And a frozen Financial Plan
      Then they can't change the Financial Plan or any of its plans
      And they can't turn the Financial Plan back into a draft
      And they can't delete the Financial Plan

  Rule: A Plan Writer can change a frozen Financial Plan's name and note

    Example: A plan is noted as reviewed with the board
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan
      Then they can change its note to "Reviewed with the board"

    Example: A frozen plan is renamed after the board adopts it
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan
      Then they can change its name to "2027 budget as adopted"
      And its P&L stays the same

  Rule: A Program Plan holds only its own program area's programs

    Example: A plan with another program area's program gets a warning
      Given a Program Plan
      And an offering in it of a program from another program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that the offering is in the wrong plan

  Rule: Merchandise, storage, and charters are planned in the Operations Plan

    Example: The Operations Plan sells merchandise
      Given the Operations Plan sells $1,000 of merchandise
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the organization's totals include $1,000 of merchandise sales

  Rule: Every program belongs to a program area

    Example: A program with no program area gets a warning
      Given a program with no program area
      And a plan with an offering of that program
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that the program has no program area

  Rule: An offering counts in full in the plan it's in, even when its dates run past that plan's year
    A winter program usually goes in the plan for the year most of its sessions fall in. The
    organization books on a cash basis, so fees paid in December show up in the books a year before
    the plan counts them. Budget-against-actuals tracking deals with that gap, not the plan.

    Example: A winter program from December to March counts in full in the 2027 plan
      Given a winter offering in the 2027 plan, from December 2026 to March 2027, that takes in $10,000
      And a practice on December 7, 2026 from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 coaches paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering takes in $10,000
      And the offering pays $120 of coaches' wages

  Rule: A program area's P&L adds up its offerings and the costs charged to the area

    Example: A program area's P&L includes its storage
      Given a program area with two offerings that each take in $10,000
      And $3,000 of storage charged to the program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the program area takes in $20,000
      And its costs include the $3,000 of storage

  Rule: A program area's P&L is also shown for each term, with offerings in no term on their own

    Example: A program area's P&L for each term
      Given a program area with an offering in "Summer 2027" that takes in $10,000
      And an offering in "Fall 2027" that takes in $4,000
      And an offering in no term that takes in $2,000
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the program area's P&L for each term is:
        | term        | takes in |
        | Summer 2027 | $10,000  |
        | Fall 2027   | $4,000   |
        | no term     | $2,000   |

    Example: Each term pays its own indirect costs
      Given a program area with an offering in "Summer 2027" and one in "Fall 2027", each costing $1,000
      And $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And every program's indirect cost rate is 50%
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the program area's P&L for "Summer 2027" includes $500 of the office costs

  Rule: A cost charged to a program area is shared among the area's offerings by their direct costs

    Example: Storage is split two to one when one offering's direct costs are twice the other's
      Given a program area with two offerings, the first with twice the direct costs of the second
      And $3,000 of storage charged to the program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the first offering pays $2,000 of the storage
      And the second offering pays $1,000 of the storage

    Example: A salaried director's hours charged to a program area are shared among its offerings
      Given a program area with two offerings with the same direct costs
      And a salaried director paid $50 an hour, with 100 hours charged to the program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then each offering pays $2,500 of the director's salary

  Rule: A charge set as a share of revenue follows each offering's price and enrollment

    Scenario Outline: Scholarships are 2% of a program's revenue
      Given the Operations Plan sets scholarships at 2% of programs' revenue
      And an offering of a program at <price> a sailor, with <sailors> sailors
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering's scholarships are <scholarships>

      Examples:
        | price | sailors | scholarships |
        | $500  | 20      | $200         |
        | $600  | 20      | $240         |
        | $500  | 10      | $100         |

    Example: A charge applies only to the kinds of product it names
      Given the Operations Plan sets scholarships at 2% of programs' revenue
      And the Operations Plan sells $1,000 of merchandise
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the merchandise has no scholarships

  Rule: Card fees that families pay are revenue, and the card company's fees are a cost

    Example: An offering collects card fees from families and pays them to the card company
      Given the Operations Plan collects card fees from families at 3% of revenue
      And the Operations Plan pays card fees at 3% of revenue
      And an offering of a program that takes in $10,000
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering takes in $300 of card fees from families
      And the offering pays $300 of card fees

  Rule: A charge set as a share of revenue counts toward the split of a program area's costs only if the Operations Plan says so

    Example: Scholarships don't change an offering's share of the storage
      Given two offerings with the same direct costs
      And scholarships on the first offering only, which don't count toward the split
      And $1,000 of storage charged to the program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then each offering pays $500 of the storage

  Rule: Capital is never charged to a program area or an offering

    Example: Buying a coach boat shows only in the organization's totals
      Given the Operations Plan buys a $20,000 coach boat
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then no offering or program area pays for the coach boat
      And the organization's totals show $20,000 of capital

    Example: A coach boat charged to a program area goes to the organization's totals instead
      Given a $20,000 coach boat charged to a program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the program area doesn't pay for the coach boat
      And the organization's totals show $20,000 of capital
      And the planner is warned that capital can't be charged to a program area

  Rule: The organization's totals show the operating net, then fundraising and capital, then the total

    Example: Fundraising pays for capital below the operating net
      Given programs that take in $100,000 and cost $90,000
      And donations of $30,000, which cost $5,000 to raise
      And the Operations Plan buys a $20,000 coach boat
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the organization's totals are:
        | line            | amount   |
        | operating net   | $10,000  |
        | fundraising net | $25,000  |
        | capital         | -$20,000 |
        | total           | $15,000  |

  Rule: The Operations Plan can pay a director a bonus on a program area's programs

    Example: A director's bonus on their area's programs
      Given the Operations Plan pays a program area's director 50% of its programs' net
      And its programs make $10,000 after their costs and shared costs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's bonus is $5,000

  Rule: A bonus is figured on the net after shared costs, or before them, as the plan says

    Scenario Outline: A director's bonus before and after shared costs
      Given a director who earns 50% of their programs' net <counted>
      And their programs take in $30,000 and cost $20,000, with an indirect cost rate of 20%
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's bonus is <bonus>

      Examples:
        | counted             | bonus  |
        | after shared costs  | $3,000 |
        | before shared costs | $5,000 |

  Rule: A bonus is paid on each term's net on its own, so a loss in one term doesn't lower another term's bonus

    Example: A director's bonus when the fall term loses money
      Given the Operations Plan pays a program area's director 10% of its programs' net
      And its programs make $20,000 in "Summer 2027" and lose $5,000 in "Fall 2027"
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's bonus is $2,000
      And the director's bonus for each term is:
        | term        | net     | bonus  |
        | Summer 2027 | $20,000 | $2,000 |
        | Fall 2027   | -$5,000 | $0     |

    Example: The bonus on offerings in no term is worked out on the year
      Given the Operations Plan pays a program area's director 10% of its programs' net
      And its programs make $20,000 in "Summer 2027" and $3,000 in no term
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's bonus for each term is:
        | term        | net     | bonus  |
        | Summer 2027 | $20,000 | $2,000 |
        | no term     | $3,000  | $300   |

    Example: A term's bonus is a cost of that term
      Given the Operations Plan pays a program area's director 10% of its programs' net
      And its programs make $20,000 in "Summer 2027" and $10,000 in "Fall 2027"
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the program area's P&L for "Summer 2027" includes $2,000 of bonus
      And the program area's P&L for "Fall 2027" includes $1,000 of bonus

  Rule: The organization pays each tax on wages on a bonus, but no L&I, since a bonus pays for no hours

    Example: A bonus costs the taxes on wages, but no L&I
      Given a payroll tax of 10% of wages
      And L&I of $0.50 for each hour worked
      And a director whose bonus is $5,000
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the organization pays $500 of payroll tax on the bonus

  Rule: A bonus and its payroll tax don't count toward the split of a program area's costs

    Example: A director's bonus doesn't change an offering's share of the storage
      Given two offerings with the same direct costs
      And a director's bonus, with its payroll tax, on the first offering only
      And $1,000 of storage charged to the program area
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then each offering pays $500 of the storage

  Rule: Each payroll tax is its own line on the P&L, charged the way it's collected

    Example: A tax on wages is a share of what the coaches are paid
      Given Social Security of 6.2% of wages
      And an offering with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 coaches paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $7.44 of Social Security

    Example: L&I is charged for each hour a coach is paid
      Given L&I of $0.50 for each hour worked
      And an offering with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 coaches paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $3.00 of L&I

    Example: A volunteer's hours cost no L&I
      Given L&I of $0.50 for each hour worked
      And an offering with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 coaches paid $0 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays no L&I

  Rule: A salaried person's hours cost L&I where their salary is charged

    Example: An offering pays L&I on its salaried coach's hours
      Given L&I of $0.50 for each hour worked
      And a salaried director paid $40 an hour
      And an offering with 10 practices of 3 hours, each in a block the director coaches
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $15.00 of L&I

  Rule: The P&L costs only the staff and boats each block is given

    Example: A block's coaches are paid for its time
      Given an offering with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 coaches paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $120 of coaches' wages

    Example: A block with fewer coaches than the program's settings call for costs only what it has
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 5 sailors
      And a Guppies offering of 10 sailors, with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 1 coach paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $60 of coaches' wages

  Rule: A block pays every coach it's given, however many sailors are expected

    Scenario Outline: A block given 4 coaches for 20 Guppies
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 5 sailors
      And a Guppies offering for up to 20 sailors that expects <expected>, with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 4 coaches paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $240 of coaches' wages

      Examples:
        | expected |
        | 10       |
        | 20       |

    Example: A salaried coach and an hourly coach on one block
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 5 sailors
      And a salaried director paid $40 an hour
      And a Guppies offering for up to 10 sailors that expects 5, with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block the director coaches with a coach paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $120 of the director's salary
      And the offering pays $60 of coaches' wages

  Rule: A block's boats, its own and chartered, cost what it's given, however many sailors are expected

    Example: 8 FJs for 16 sailors, when 10 are expected
      Given FJs that cost $1 an hour on the water in upkeep
      And an offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 8 of the organization's FJs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $24 of boat upkeep

    Example: 2 RIBs for 16 sailors, when 5 are expected
      Given RIBs that cost $6 an hour in fuel and $4 an hour in upkeep
      And an offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 5, with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 of the organization's RIBs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $60 for coach boats

    Example: 8 chartered FJs for 16 sailors, when 10 are expected
      Given FJs that charter for $50 a day
      And an offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 8 chartered FJs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $400 for boat charters

  Rule: The offerings in a block share its costs by their number of sailors

    Example: Two offerings share a coach
      Given two offerings, of 6 sailors and 4 sailors, in one block from 9am to noon with 1 coach paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the first offering pays $36 of coaches' wages
      And the second offering pays $24 of coaches' wages

  Rule: A block runs from its first activity's start to its last activity's end

    Example: Two practices that overlap in one block
      Given two offerings in one block with 1 coach paid $20 an hour
      And one practices from 9am to 11am and the other from 10am to noon
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the two offerings pay $60 of coaches' wages between them

  Rule: The organization's coach boats cost fuel and upkeep for their time on the water

    Example: Two RIBs for a morning practice
      Given RIBs that cost $6 an hour in fuel and $4 an hour in upkeep
      And an offering with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 2 of the organization's RIBs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $60 for coach boats

  Rule: A chartered boat costs its class's charter rate for each day it's chartered

    Example: A RIB chartered for a morning and an afternoon costs one day
      Given RIBs that charter for $150 a day
      And a block with 1 chartered RIB on the morning of July 13, and another with 1 that afternoon
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the two offerings pay $150 for boat charters between them

  Rule: The organization's boats cost upkeep for their time on the water, and boats that sailors bring cost nothing

    Example: Four of the organization's Optis and three that sailors bring
      Given Optis that cost $1 an hour on the water in upkeep
      And an offering with a practice from 9am to noon, in a block with 4 of the organization's Optis and 3 Optis that sailors bring
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $12 of boat upkeep

  Rule: A salaried coach on a block is paid out of their salary for its hours

    Example: An offering pays for its salaried coach's hours
      Given a salaried director paid $40 an hour
      And an offering with 10 practices of 3 hours, each in a block the director coaches
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $1,200 of the director's salary

  Rule: A salaried coach can be paid by the hour for coaching, at their own hourly rate, on top of their salary

    Example: A director is paid by the hour for the blocks they coach
      Given a director on a $20,000 salary, who's paid $30 an hour to coach
      And an offering with 10 practices of 3 hours, each in a block the director coaches by the hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $900 of coaches' wages
      And the offering pays none of the director's salary

    Example: The block's other coaches are paid their staff level's rate
      Given a director on a $20,000 salary, who's paid $40 an hour to coach
      And an offering with 10 practices of 3 hours, each in a block the director coaches by the hour with a coach paid $30 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays $2,100 of coaches' wages

    Example: A director with no hourly rate of their own is paid nothing for those hours
      Given a director on a $20,000 salary, with no hourly rate to coach
      And an offering with 10 practices of 3 hours, each in a block the director coaches by the hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays no coaches' wages
      And the planner is warned that the director has no hourly rate to coach

  Rule: A salaried person's pay for the year adds up their salary, their hourly coaching, and their bonus

    Example: A director's pay for the year
      Given a director on a $20,000 salary, who's paid $30 an hour to coach
      And an offering with 10 practices of 3 hours, each in a block the director coaches by the hour
      And a bonus for the director that comes to $5,000
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's pay for the year is:
        | pay             | amount  |
        | salary          | $20,000 |
        | hourly coaching | $900    |
        | bonus           | $5,000  |
        | total           | $25,900 |

  Rule: A salaried coach can't coach two blocks at the same time

    Example: Two blocks at the same time get a warning
      Given a salaried director who coaches two blocks
      And both blocks run from 4pm to 7pm on the same day
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that the director is coaching two blocks at once

    Example: A morning block and an afternoon block don't get a warning
      Given a salaried director who coaches two blocks
      And one block runs from 9am to noon and the other from 1pm to 4pm on the same day
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about the director

  Rule: A planner sees how many hours of another coach a double-booked salaried coach needs

    Example: Two blocks overlap for two hours
      Given a salaried director who coaches two blocks
      And one block runs from 9am to noon and the other from 10am to 2pm on the same day
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's blocks need 2 hours of another coach

    Example: Three blocks at once need two more coaches
      Given a salaried director who coaches three blocks
      And all three blocks run from 4pm to 7pm on the same day
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the director's blocks need 6 hours of another coach

  Rule: A planner is warned when more of the organization's boats are out at once than it has that day

    Example: 14 Sailqubes out on a morning when the organization has 12
      Given the organization has 12 Sailqubes
      And a Guppies block with 9 Sailqubes and a Minnows block with 5, both on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that July 13 has 14 Sailqubes out, but the organization has 12

    Example: A morning block and an afternoon block can use the same boats
      Given the organization has 12 Sailqubes
      And a Guppies block with 9 Sailqubes on the morning of July 13, and a Minnows block with 5 that afternoon
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about Sailqubes

    Example: Boats that sailors bring don't count against the organization's
      Given the organization has 12 Optis
      And a block with 10 of the organization's Optis and 4 Optis that sailors bring
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about Optis

    Example: Chartered boats don't count against the organization's
      Given the organization has 6 RIBs
      And a block on the morning of July 13 with 6 of the organization's RIBs and 2 chartered RIBs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about RIBs

    Example: 7 RIBs out on a morning when the organization has 6
      Given the organization has 6 RIBs
      And two blocks on the morning of July 13, with 4 RIBs and 3 RIBs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that July 13 has 7 RIBs out, but the organization has 6

    Example: A class a program can sail stands in for the one it's given
      Given Guppies can sail Sailqubes and FJs
      And the organization has 12 Sailqubes and 2 FJs
      And a Guppies block with 9 Sailqubes and a Minnows block with 5, both on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about Sailqubes

    Example: Any of the organization's powerboats can be a coach boat
      Given the organization has 6 RIBs and 1 support boat
      And two blocks on the morning of July 13, with 4 RIBs and 3 RIBs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about RIBs

    Example: Boats the organization gets before the day count
      Given the organization has 12 Sailqubes, and gets 2 more on July 1
      And a Guppies block with 9 Sailqubes and a Minnows block with 5, both on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about Sailqubes

    Example: Boats the organization gets after the day don't count yet
      Given the organization has 12 Sailqubes, and gets 2 more on August 1
      And a Guppies block with 9 Sailqubes and a Minnows block with 5, both on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that July 13 has 14 Sailqubes out, but the organization has 12

    Example: Boats the organization disposed of before the day don't count
      Given the organization has 14 Sailqubes, and disposes of 2 on July 1
      And a Guppies block with 9 Sailqubes and a Minnows block with 5, both on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that July 13 has 14 Sailqubes out, but the organization has 12

  Rule: A planner is warned when the organization hasn't enough boats for every offering to fill

    Example: Sailqubes for 10 Guppies, when 16 can sign up
      Given Sailqubes take 2 sailors each
      And the organization has 6 Sailqubes
      And a Guppies offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, in a block with 5 Sailqubes on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that on July 13 Guppies needs 8 Sailqubes when it's full, but the organization has 6

    Example: Boats that sailors bring count
      Given Sailqubes take 2 sailors each
      And the organization has 6 Sailqubes
      And a Guppies offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, in a block on the morning of July 13 with 5 of the organization's Sailqubes and 2 that sailors bring
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned that the organization hasn't enough Sailqubes

    Example: A block short of seats needs only enough boats to seat them
      Given Harpoons take 6 sailors each
      And RS Ventures take 6 sailors each
      And the organization has 3 Harpoons and 2 RS Ventures
      And a Guppies offering for up to 26 sailors that expects 20, in a block with 2 Harpoons and 2 RS Ventures on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned that the organization hasn't enough RS Ventures

    Example: A coach boat for each coach a full program needs
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 5 sailors
      And the organization has 8 Sailqubes and 3 RIBs
      And a Guppies offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, in a block with 8 Sailqubes and 2 RIBs on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that on July 13 Guppies needs 4 RIBs when it's full, but the organization has 3

    Example: Too few Sailqubes even for the sailors expected is an error
      Given Sailqubes take 2 sailors each
      And the organization has 4 Sailqubes
      And a Guppies offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, in a block with 4 Sailqubes on the morning of July 13
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is shown an error that on July 13 Guppies needs 5 Sailqubes for the sailors expected, but the organization has 4

    Example: Too few coaches for a full program isn't a warning
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 10 sailors
      And a Guppies offering for up to 16 sailors that expects 10, in a block with one coach and enough boats for 16
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner isn't warned about the block

  Rule: A planner sees the most sailors each offering's block can take

    Example: Two coaches for 16 Guppies, but boats for only 12
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 8 sailors
      And Sailqubes take 2 sailors each
      And a Guppies offering for up to 16 sailors, in a block with 2 coaches and 6 Sailqubes
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering's block can take 12 sailors

    Example: A block shared with another program leaves room for that program's full number
      Given a block for up to 10 Guppies and up to 5 Minnows, with boats for 40 sailors
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the Guppies offering's block can take 35 sailors
      And the Minnows offering's block can take 30 sailors

  Rule: A planner sees the fewest sailors at which an offering pays for itself

    Example: $500 a sailor against $3,000 of costs
      Given an offering for up to 16 sailors at $500 a sailor, with $3,000 of its own costs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays for itself at 6 sailors

    Example: It pays its indirect costs on its direct costs
      Given $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And an offering for up to 16 sailors at $500 a sailor, with $2,000 of its own costs
      And every program's indirect cost rate is 50%
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays for itself at 6 sailors

    Example: Every coach on the block is paid, however many sailors sign up
      Given Guppies have a coach for every 5 sailors
      And a Guppies offering for up to 10 sailors at $50 a sailor, with $100 of its own costs and a practice from 9am to noon in a block with 2 coaches paid $20 an hour
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering pays for itself at 5 sailors

    Example: An offering that loses money even when it's full
      Given an offering for up to 10 sailors at $100 a sailor, with $2,000 of its own costs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the offering doesn't pay for itself, even when it's full

  Rule: A frozen Financial Plan keeps its warnings, each with the plan and date it's about

    Example: A practice on a closed day in a frozen Financial Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan whose Program Plan has a practice on August 2, a closed day
      When they freeze the Financial Plan
      Then the Financial Plan's warnings say that the practice on August 2, in its Program Plan, is on a closed day

  Rule: A planner is warned about an activity on a closed day

    Example: A practice on Labor Day gets a warning
      Given the Operations Plan lists Labor Day as a closed day
      And an offering with a practice on Labor Day
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the planner is warned that the practice is on a closed day

  Rule: Each program pays its indirect cost rate on its direct costs, and the organization keeps the rest

    Example: Two Program Plans pay their rates, and the organization keeps what's unallocated
      Given $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And a draft Financial Plan with two Program Plans whose offerings each cost $1,000
      And every program's indirect cost rate is 30%
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then each Program Plan's offering pays $300 of the office costs
      And the organization's totals show $400 of unallocated indirect costs

    Example: Storage doesn't count toward a program's indirect costs
      Given $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And a program area with two offerings with the same direct costs
      And $3,000 of storage charged to the program area
      And every program's indirect cost rate is 10%
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then each offering pays $100 of the office costs

    Example: One program's year doesn't change what another pays
      Given $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And a program area with two offerings with the same direct costs
      And every program's indirect cost rate is 30%
      When the second offering costs twice as much
      Then the first offering still pays $300 of the office costs

    Example: The full rate is the shared costs over every program's direct costs
      Given $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And a program area with two offerings that each cost $2,000
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then the full rate, which would allocate every shared cost, is 25%

    Example: A program with no indirect cost rate pays none of the shared costs
      Given $1,000 of office costs shared by every offering
      And a program area with two offerings with the same direct costs
      When a planner calculates the P&L
      Then no offering pays the office costs
      And the planner is warned that a program has no indirect cost rate

  Rule: Only a Plan Admin sets a program's indirect cost rate, since a director's bonus counts it

    Example: A Plan Writer can't set a program's indirect cost rate
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan
      Then they can't set the indirect cost rate of one of a Program Plan's programs

    Example: A Plan Admin sets a program's indirect cost rate
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan
      Then they can set the indirect cost rate of one of a Program Plan's programs

  Rule: A draft's P&L is up to date after every change to it

    Example: A planner's AI changes a price, then sees the new P&L
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan whose offering takes in $10,000
      When they change the offering to take in $12,000
      Then they see that the Financial Plan's P&L shows the offering takes in $12,000

  Rule: A frozen Financial Plan keeps the P&L it had when it was frozen

    Example: A frozen Financial Plan shows the P&L it was frozen with
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan whose offering takes in $10,000
      When they freeze the Financial Plan
      Then the Financial Plan's P&L shows that the offering takes in $10,000

    Example: A Financial Plan started from it doesn't change its P&L
      Given a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" whose offering pays $1,000 of office costs
      When a planner freezes a Financial Plan started from "Budget" with twice the indirect cost rate
      Then the P&L of "Budget" still shows that the offering pays $1,000 of office costs

    Example: A frozen Financial Plan keeps each term's P&L and bonus
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a draft Financial Plan that pays a director 10% of an offering in "Summer 2027" that takes in $10,000
      When they freeze the Financial Plan
      Then the Financial Plan's P&L shows that the program area takes in $10,000 in "Summer 2027"
      And the Financial Plan's P&L shows that the director's bonus for "Summer 2027" is $1,000

  Rule: Each line of a frozen P&L says in plain words what it is

    Example: An offering's share of the office is named for its pool and its line
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And $1,000 of office costs in a pool named "G&A"
      And a draft Financial Plan whose offering costs $10,000
      And every program's indirect cost rate is 10%
      When they freeze the Financial Plan
      Then the Financial Plan's P&L names the offering's share of the office "G&A share: Office"

  Rule: No one can write a Financial Plan's P&L by hand, even an admin

    Example: A Plan Writer can't write a Financial Plan's P&L
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      Then they can't change a frozen Financial Plan's P&L
      And they can't add a P&L to a draft Financial Plan

    Example: An admin can't write a Financial Plan's P&L either
      Given a user with the Admin role
      Then they can't change a frozen Financial Plan's P&L
      And they can't add a P&L to a draft Financial Plan

  Rule: A Plan Writer can write a plan's notes in a draft Financial Plan, and they never change once it's frozen

    Example: A Plan Writer writes a Program Plan's notes
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan
      Then they can write a Program Plan's notes

    Example: A Plan Writer writes a program's notes
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan
      Then they can write the notes for one of a Program Plan's programs

    Example: A Plan Writer can't change the notes in a frozen Financial Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan whose Program Plan has notes
      Then they can't change the Program Plan's notes

  Rule: Each of a plan's notes is one short line

    Example: A note longer than 280 characters
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a draft Financial Plan
      When they try to give a Program Plan a note longer than 280 characters
      Then they're told that each note is one line of at most 280 characters

  Rule: A planner keeps the draft their AI filled in by freezing it, or rejects it by deleting it

    Example: A planner freezes the draft their AI filled in
      Given a user with the Plan Writer and Plan Admin policies
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget"
      And a draft their AI started from "Budget", with its blocks filled in and a note of what it changed
      When they freeze the draft
      Then the draft is frozen with its blocks, its note, and its P&L
      And its parent is "Budget"

    Example: Deleting the draft leaves the plan it was started from as it was
      Given a user with the Plan Writer policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan named "Budget" with an offering at $1,000 a sailor
      And a draft their AI started from "Budget", with its blocks filled in and a note of what it changed
      When they delete the draft
      Then the draft is gone
      And "Budget" still has the offering at $1,000 a sailor

  Rule: Planners can see every plan, including wages and bonus rates

    Scenario Outline: Each planning policy can see a plan's wages and bonus rates
      Given a user with the <policy> policy
      And a plan with wages and bonus rates
      Then they can see every part of the plan, wages and bonus rates included

      Examples:
        | policy        |
        | Plan Reader   |
        | Plan Writer   |
        | Plan Admin    |

  Rule: A Plan Reader can't change plans

    Example: A Plan Reader can't change a plan
      Given a user with the Plan Reader policy
      Then they can't change plans

  Rule: A Plan Reader can only look at a Financial Plan

    Example: A Plan Reader looks at a Financial Plan
      Given a user with the Plan Reader policy
      And a frozen Financial Plan
      Then they can see the Financial Plan and its P&L
      But they can't start, freeze, or make a Financial Plan active

  Rule: Only admins and planners can see plans

    Scenario Outline: A role on its own doesn't let someone see plans
      Given a user with the <role> role
      Then they can't see plans

      Examples:
        | role      |
        | Community |
        | Crew      |

    Scenario Outline: Other policies don't let someone see plans
      Given a user with the <policy> policy
      Then they can't see plans

      Examples:
        | policy         |
        | CRM Writer     |
        | Catalog Reader |
        | Catalog Writer |
